---
import MethodSection from "./MethodSection.astro";

const criteria = [
  {
    label: "Uniform fraction",
    detail:
      "At least 80% of per-question model_distribution entries are within 0.01 of perfectly uniform (e.g. {0.25, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25} for a 4-option question).",
  },
  {
    label: "Negligible refusals",
    detail:
      "Mean per-question model_refusal_rate ≤ 0.05. A model that actually refuses most questions is showing a legitimate safety pattern, not a failed API response.",
  },
  {
    label: "Enough questions",
    detail:
      "At least 10 questions in the run. Shorter runs don't give enough signal to tell real flat distributions apart from ones caused by API failures.",
  },
];
---

<MethodSection id="run-validity" title="Run validity filtering" fold>
  <p class="sb-muted sb-measure" slot="lede">
    Silent API failures (budget exhaustion, missed model aliases, provider fallbacks) can return responses
    that parse cleanly but carry no real signal. The result is a run whose per-question distributions are
    all perfectly uniform. Parse-failure tracking does not catch this case, because the responses did
    parse. We filter these runs out at publish time so they never reach the leaderboard.
  </p>

  <div class="sb-prose">
    <p>A run is excluded when <strong class="sb-ink">all three</strong> of the following hold:</p>
    <ul class="sb-mlist">
      {
        criteria.map((c) => (
          <li>
            <strong class="sb-ink">{c.label}.</strong> {c.detail}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p>
      Excluded runs are listed in <code class="sb-mcode">leaderboard.json#excluded_runs</code> for
      transparency, and operators can run <code class="sb-mcode">synthbench scan-invalid</code> locally against
      any results directory. The rule is deliberately strict: we would rather miss a borderline case than
      wrongly exclude a legitimate run whose distributions happen to be flat.
    </p>
  </div>
</MethodSection>
